.Dd November 12, 2026
.Dt FBDRM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fbdrm ,
.Nm simpledrm
.Nd DRM driver of the framebuffer the boot loader leaves
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In drm/drm.h
.In drm/drm_mode.h
.Pp
.Pa /dev/dri/cardN
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver drives the screen GRUB sets up before it starts the kernel, as
Linux 6.12's simpledrm drives a firmware framebuffer.
It binds when the loader's boot information describes a direct-colour
framebuffer the kernel can reach, in one of the layouts simpledrm knows:
RGB565, XRGB1555, RGB888, XRGB8888, XBGR8888 or XRGB2101010.
A machine booted by UEFI always has one; a BIOS boot has one when the
entry asks for a graphics mode, and none in text mode.
.Pp
A native driver of the same screen takes it first.
The kernel probes the native drivers, such as
.Xr bochs 4 ,
before
.Nm ,
and a native driver removes the firmware's framebuffer from the ranges its
device decodes; then
.Nm
binds nothing and the kernel logs
.Dq fbdrm: framebuffer at ADDRESS is a native driver's .
.Cm nomodeset
on the kernel command line keeps the native drivers off,
.Xr bochs 4 ,
.Xr virtio-gpu 4
and
.Xr i915 4 ,
and leaves the screen to
.Nm ,
as on Linux, unless a driver's
.Cm modeset
parameter gives another value than \-1.
.Pp
A bound framebuffer is a DRM device, as
.Xr drm 4
describes, registered as
.Ql simpledrm
with Linux's identity: description
.Ql DRM driver for simple-framebuffer platform devices ,
version 1.0.0 and date 20200625, on the platform bus as
.Ql simple-framebuffer.0 .
It has a card node alone, and the features
.Dv DRIVER_GEM ,
.Dv DRIVER_MODESET
and
.Dv DRIVER_ATOMIC .
The kernel logs
.Dq fbdrm: registered
with its size, format, pitch, address and card.
.Ss Display
The device has one CRTC, one primary plane, one encoder of type
.Dv DRM_MODE_ENCODER_NONE
and one connector of type
.Dv DRM_MODE_CONNECTOR_Unknown ,
.Ql Unknown-1 ,
always connected.
The connector has one mode, the framebuffer's size at 60 Hz with no
blanking, preferred, and a physical size reckoned at 96 dots an inch, as
simpledrm gives them.
The mode cannot change: a commit of another size fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
The plane takes the framebuffer's own format and XRGB8888.
Framebuffers are dumb buffers in system memory, at least as wide as the
screen and at most 4096 pixels or the screen's width, whichever is more.
A commit copies the framebuffer onto the screen, converting from XRGB8888
to the screen's format: the damage clips the commit names, through
.Dv FB_DAMAGE_CLIPS
or
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_MODE_DIRTYFB ,
or the whole plane when it names none.
Turning the plane off paints the screen black.
.Ss Choosing the mode
The mode is the one the loader set, so it is chosen in the GRUB entry that
starts the kernel.
On a BIOS machine,
.Va gfxpayload ,
set after the
.Ic multiboot
or
.Ic multiboot2
command, names it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
multiboot2 /boot/kernel rach2.root=...
set gfxpayload=1366x768x32
boot
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql auto
asks the video BIOS for the screen's preferred mode, a list such as
.Ql 1920x1080,1366x768
takes the first the video BIOS has, and
.Ql text
leaves the screen in text mode, with no framebuffer and so no
.Nm .
Under UEFI the framebuffer is the one the firmware set up, whatever the
variable says.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/dri/cardN
.It Pa /dev/dri/cardN
The card node of the bound framebuffer.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bochs 4 ,
.Xr console 4 ,
.Xr drm 4 ,
.Xr i915 4 ,
.Xr virtio-gpu 4 ,
.Xr drm-kms 7
.Sh HISTORY
The driver follows Linux 6.12's simpledrm, whose identity, mode, formats
and copy it takes.
